{"causes": ["Edward I of England's expansionist policies and claim of suzerainty over Scotland directly provoked Scottish resistance among the nobility.", "The inherent instability of the Scottish succession crisis following the death of Margaret, Maid of Norway, created a power vacuum exploited by England.", "The feudal obligations and shifting loyalties of Scottish magnates like Douglas were continually tested by the escalating conflict with the English crown."], "effects": ["William the Hardy's defiance and death in English captivity solidified his image as an early martyr for Scottish independence.", "His son, James Douglas, inherited a legacy of resistance and went on to become one of the most celebrated commanders of Robert the Bruce.", "The Douglas family's prominence and influence in Scottish affairs were significantly bolstered by William's actions during the Wars of Independence."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas", "sourceName": "William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "scottish-wars-of-independence", "targetName": "Scottish Wars of Independence", "context": "William the Hardy was an early and persistent participant in the First War of Scottish Independence against England from 1296 until his death in 1298."}, {"sourceSlug": "edward-i-of-england", "sourceName": "Edward I of England", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas", "targetName": "William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "context": "Edward I's aggressive claims of suzerainty over Scotland and subsequent invasion in 1296 directly caused William the Hardy to rebel."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas", "sourceName": "William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "william-wallace", "targetName": "William Wallace", "context": "William the Hardy joined forces with William Wallace in the Scottish insurgency against English rule in 1297, prior to his final capture."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas", "sourceName": "William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "berwick-upon-tweed-scotland", "targetName": "Berwick-upon-Tweed, Scotland", "context": "William the Hardy captured Berwick Castle from the English in 1296, marking a significant act of rebellion against Edward I."}, {"sourceSlug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas", "sourceName": "William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "james-douglas-the-black-douglas", "targetName": "James Douglas, 'the Black Douglas'", "context": "The defiant spirit and ultimate sacrifice of William the Hardy profoundly influenced his son, James Douglas, who became a key commander for Robert the Bruce."}], "places": [{"name": "Berwick-upon-Tweed, Scotland", "role": "Site of capture"}, {"name": "Tower of London, England", "role": "Place of death"}, {"name": "Douglas, Scotland", "role": "Ancestral seat"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: William the Hardy, Lord of Douglas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "william-the-hardy-lord-of-douglas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1123011", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_le_Hardi,_Lord_of_Douglas"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "William the Hardy's early and persistent defiance against Edward I and his death in captivity solidified his place as an early martyr for Scottish independence, directly influencing the subsequent resistance led by his son and Robert the Bruce and establishing the prominence of the Douglas clan.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-28T07:22:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33148608363", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Sir William Douglas "le Hardi", Lord of Douglas was a Scottish nobleman and soldier.", "newValue": "Sir William Douglas "le Hardi", Lord of Douglas (died 1298), stands as a pivotal early figure in the Scottish Wars of Independence, whose defiance against Edward I of England solidified the nascent spirit of resistance among the Scottish nobility. As the first recorded Lord of Douglas, he established the formidable reputation of one of Scotland's most powerful and enduring aristocratic families. His actions, marked by shifting allegiances and steadfast opposition to English annexation, placed him at the heart of the tumultuous Anglo-Scottish relations in the late 13th century, directly influencing the political landscape that would give rise to national heroes like William Wallace and Robert the Bruce.

Douglas's active resistance commenced dramatically in 1296 when, following Edward I's invasion of Scotland, he famously captured Berwick Castle from the English, an act of overt rebellion against the English crown which had by then claimed suzerainty over Scotland. His subsequent oath of fealty to Edward I, known as the Ragman Roll, proved short-lived, as he swiftly joined William Wallace's insurgency. This renewed defiance led to his capture by English forces at the Capitulation of Irvine in 1297, alongside other Scottish nobles. Despite further oaths and paroles, his consistent refusal to submit fully to English rule defined his public life and military career.

His ultimate fate, death in the Tower of London in 1298, transformed him into an early martyr for Scottish independence, a symbol of unyielding national spirit. William the Hardy's legacy was profoundly carried forward by his son, James Douglas, known as "the Black Douglas," who became one of Robert the Bruce's most trusted and formidable commanders. The Douglas clan, forged in the fires of these wars, would become synonymous with Scottish martial prowess and a dominant force in the nation's history for centuries, directly owing their initial prominence to the defiant stand of their progenitor."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-28T07:22:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33148608363", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1243, Scotland"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1243, Scotland"}], "newValue": ["Edward I of England's expansionist policies and claim of suzerainty over Scotland directly provoked Scottish resistance among the nobility.", "The inherent instability of the Scottish succession crisis following the death of Margaret, Maid of Norway, created a power vacuum exploited by England.", "The feudal obligations and shifting loyalties of Scottish magnates like Douglas were continually tested by the escalating conflict with the English crown."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-28T07:22:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33148608363", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1298"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1298"}], "newValue": ["William the Hardy's defiance and death in English captivity solidified his image as an early martyr for Scottish independenc